a:
Book ↔ author is many-to-many. A loan is a fact with a time range. The "one holder at a time" rule is enforced by a **partial unique index**, not by app code:

```sql
CREATE TABLE author (id bigserial PRIMARY KEY, name text NOT NULL);
CREATE TABLE reader (id bigserial PRIMARY KEY, full_name text NOT NULL);
CREATE TABLE book   (id bigserial PRIMARY KEY, title text NOT NULL, isbn text UNIQUE);
CREATE TABLE book_author (
    book_id   bigint NOT NULL REFERENCES book(id) ON DELETE CASCADE,
    author_id bigint NOT NULL REFERENCES author(id),
    PRIMARY KEY (book_id, author_id)
);
CREATE TABLE loan (
    id          bigserial PRIMARY KEY,
    book_id     bigint NOT NULL REFERENCES book(id),
    reader_id   bigint NOT NULL REFERENCES reader(id),
    taken_at    timestamptz NOT NULL DEFAULT now(),
    returned_at timestamptz
);
CREATE UNIQUE INDEX loan_one_active_per_book ON loan(book_id) WHERE returned_at IS NULL;

-- books currently on loan
SELECT b.title FROM book b JOIN loan l ON l.book_id = b.id WHERE l.returned_at IS NULL;

-- books with more than 3 authors
SELECT b.title FROM book b JOIN book_author ba ON ba.book_id = b.id
GROUP BY b.id, b.title HAVING COUNT(*) > 3;

-- top-3 authors by books currently on loan
SELECT a.name, COUNT(*) AS on_loan
FROM loan l
JOIN book_author ba ON ba.book_id = l.book_id
JOIN author a ON a.id = ba.author_id
WHERE l.returned_at IS NULL
GROUP BY a.id, a.name
ORDER BY on_loan DESC
LIMIT 3;
```

A second active loan fails with `duplicate key value violates unique constraint "loan_one_active_per_book"` (verified on PG18), so concurrent checkouts are safe without explicit locks. Returning a book sets `returned_at`, and the history is kept.

With **multiple copies**: **ISBN identifies an edition, not a physical copy**. Add `book_copy(id, book_id, inventory_no)`, and put the loan and the unique index on `copy_id`. An alternative is an exclusion constraint on `tstzrange(taken_at, returned_at)` with GiST.

